{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested for exact head dfa5205f44b8b5f71c1475c3a250c58fdef17679 on task/whoami-truthful-orientation-current-main. The behaviour at this head is correct and I found no defect in shipped code. What I am asking for is small and confined to tests: the one behaviour this integration newly depends on is not held by an assertion that can fail, and the assertion that appears to cover the other half of it provably cannot fail.\n\nWHAT HOLDS\n\nExact head, parents, cleanliness. Reviewed /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/whoami-truthful-orientation-current-main at dfa5205f44b8b5f71c1475c3a250c58fdef17679, clean before and after every gate, with exact parents 346ffb108c798dd193e3324ae4753c082117d9dc and 7daddceae35b6d35a3335248f3dfced3541b2b0c, Rests-On trailer git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b23dbd618a9d5b6bed8ed31b1059faedb69aa537, git diff --check clean. Every measurement below was taken with git rev-parse HEAD in the same command and returned dfa5205f.\n\nConflict set and resolution. I re-performed the merge from 346ffb10 in a scratch worktree: exactly cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go and cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go conflict, as claimed. Stronger, the whole merged tree except those two files is byte-identical to git's automatic merge, so nothing else was touched by hand. Both sides survive in the two resolved files: main's per-call/default repository selection, room attachment by common git directory, repository-published resident discovery and restart re-lookup, errNoResident, lost(); and the candidate's orientation read, all trust guards, validateResidentURL, newResidentClient, getBoundedJSON. No test was dropped or invented: 14 test functions at the main parent, 17 at the candidate, 22 at the head, exactly the union. The only removals in the resolved test file are the widening of attachedServer from *testing.T to testing.TB and an import move; no assertion was weakened. The ui/ and internal/service/uidist trees at this head are byte-identical to current main (0239edcc871919276b14c092c1f3efbd14a76bbf), so the embed gate is inherited unchanged rather than re-derived; UI tests pass 42/42.\n\nGates at the pinned head. make vet, make test (15 packages), make race (15 packages), make build all pass; worktree clean before and after.\n\nSix guard mutations, each applied alone in a scratch worktree and restored, all CAUGHT with the right failure reason:\n- ProjectionVersion equality removed -\u003e main_test.go:822 \"untrusted resident answer was accepted\" (projection_version_mismatch).\n- Nonnegative Frontier.Depth removed -\u003e main_test.go:822 (negative_depth).\n- Required You.Name/You.Kind/You.MembershipEvent removed -\u003e main_test.go:822, all three of missing_name, missing_kind, missing_member.\n- acceptSnapshot's w.snapshotSource = SnapshotSourceIncrementalTail deleted -\u003e app_test.go:664 \"accepted local append reported \\\"verified_signed_checkpoint_tail\\\"\".\n- orientationTimeout 2s -\u003e 5s with the literal assertion relaxed to match -\u003e main_test.go:870 \"stalled resident was not bounded: elapsed=5.290523958s\". The behaviour, not the literal, is what fails.\n- orientationResponseLimit 64 KiB -\u003e 1 MiB with the literal assertion relaxed to match -\u003e main_test.go:784 \"invalid resident answer was accepted\" (oversized). Again behavioural: the oversized payload is built from an independent 64 KiB literal, not from the constant, so this is not a self-comparison.\n\nThe challenged condition is true. I proved it by direct experiment rather than by reading. A probe at this head shows a session whose first call is whoami has joined 0 rooms, and after one ordinary tool call has joined 1 room whose presence the resident reports. whoami's answer carries no presence-derived field: actor, durable, session, protocol, repo, genesis, frontier, source and degraded all come from the orientation read or from local durable evidence, and degraded is set solely by whether the resident answer validated. Skipping the announce therefore reports nothing it has not established. Within a call I could not find another path out of the bound: attach and endpoint are local, both Store.Head reads and the HTTP GET take the 2s residentContext, and the two attempts share one deadline.\n\nWHAT I AM ASKING FOR\n\n1. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go:518. The assertion\n\n\tif len(presence.(map[string]any)) == 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"the session announced no presence through the published service\")\n\t}\n\ncannot fail. GET /v0/presence returns a nexus.Snapshot, so the decoded map always has three keys (conversations, cursor, presence) whether or not anyone announced. Proof: I made every tool, not only whoami, use attach and skip the announce. TestResidentServiceIsFoundInTheRepository still PASSED. With the assertion repaired to look at the inner map it FAILS with presence:map[] and PASSES unmodified at this head. So \"other tools still announce normally\" is currently unpinned. The repair is one line:\n\n\tif live := presence.(map[string]any)[\"presence\"]; live == nil || len(live.(map[string]any)) == 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the session announced no presence through the published service: %#v\", presence)\n\t}\n\nThis assertion came in from current main unchanged, so it is not a defect this merge introduced. It becomes load-bearing here, because this merge is what makes \"which tools announce\" a decision rather than a uniformity.\n\n2. The whoami exemption itself is pinned only by a deadlock. Restoring attend for whoami does fail, but only by hanging: the announce POST in TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects runs on context.Background() against a handler that blocks until its request context is done, so the test binary dies on its timeout after 90s with a goroutine dump and no message naming the cause. That is a real failure but a poor one to inherit; on a slower machine or with a different -timeout it reads as flakiness. A short direct assertion, of the shape I used to probe it, states the property in one place and fails in under a second:\n\n\tserver.call(ctx, toolCall{Name: \"whoami\"})  -\u003e  len(server.attended()) == 0\n\tserver.call(ctx, toolCall{Name: \"presence\"}) -\u003e  len(server.attended()) == 1\n\n3. Record the exemption as its own decision. Exempting whoami from attend is a behaviour change relative to current main, where every tool announced, and it is present in neither parent. Request b23dbd61 asked for the two files to be resolved \"additively, preserving current repository-selection/serve behavior\", and this is the one place that condition is not met. I judge the change correct and necessary - my mutation shows the additive resolution deadlocks the suite - and it was disclosed rather than hidden, which is why this is a bookkeeping ask and not a rejection of the design. It should rest on a request or decision of its own so a stranger can see why whoami stopped joining, rather than finding it inside a conflict resolution.\n\nOBSERVATIONS, NOT BLOCKING\n\n- The 2s bound covers the resident read only. whoami's total latency is attach plus up to 2s plus, on the degraded path, an unbounded local SnapshotWithSource on the outer context, which for a cold full audit of a large log is real work. docs/reference.md says the boundary guards \"the answer\" from the resident, which is accurate, and the degraded source is disclosed. No overclaim, but the elapsed assertion holds only because the test workspace is small.\n- gitseq-mcp's main() still announces to the default repository on context.Background() at startup, so a stalled resident can block a fresh adapter before its first whoami. This is unchanged from both parents and out of scope here.\n- The resident answer is rejected unless You.Roles contains \"participant\". Every roster member does get participant (fold.go:648 grants it with membership), so this is safe today; if it ever were not, whoami would fall back to a truthful local answer with degraded:true rather than lie.\n\nI did not merge, edit, or ratify anything, and I removed both scratch worktrees. Nothing else in the conditions was left unchecked. If the two test repairs land on a new head I expect re-review to be quick: no guard, gate, or resolution finding stands against this work.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04847d31048847e40e7b63d00a87655044f82cb9","head":"dfa5205f44b8b5f71c1475c3a250c58fdef17679","verdict":"changes-requested"}}